\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {26}{\ignorespaces Distributional prediction error predicts graded N400/P600 amplitudes across all eight case roles. \textbf  {Left}: illustrative Gaussian ERP waveforms for three violation conditions --- congruent (NOM\ensuremath  {\to }NOM), mild (ACC\ensuremath  {\to }NOM), and strong (VOC\ensuremath  {\to }NOM) --- with fixed template amplitudes (\(-1.5/3.0\), \(-4.0/3.0\), \(-7.0/6.0\) \ensuremath  {\mitmu }V N400/P600) and peaks at 380 ms / 600 ms (matching \texttt {DEFAULT\_N400\_PEAK\_MS} / \texttt {DEFAULT\_P600\_PEAK\_MS} in \texttt {src/daif/prediction.py}), shown to depict the \emph  {mechanism} of \autoref {eq:eq-7c-n400}--\autoref {eq:eq-7c-p600} rather than a calibrated simulation. \textbf  {Middle}: scatter of enriched weight \(w\) versus scalar DPE (\autoref {eq:eq-7c-dpe-scalar}) for all eight case roles, computed via \texttt {distributional\_prediction\_error()} in \texttt {src/daif/prediction.py}. \textbf  {Right}: \emph  {real} DAIF-predicted magnitudes --- mean N400 magnitude via \texttt {n400\_from\_return\_distribution()} and mean P600 via \texttt {p600\_from\_precision\_update()} across the eight roles (\texttt {make\_erp\_prediction\_data()} in \texttt {src/cognitive/figure\_data.py}) --- alongside literature-typical amplitude ranges from Kutas \& Federmeier \citeyearpar  {kutas2011thirty} (N400 magnitude 3--5 \ensuremath  {\mitmu }V, P600 5--8 \ensuremath  {\mitmu }V, shown with error bars). Note that the model predictions are on a \emph  {dimensionless} DPE scale (units of log-probability \ensuremath  {\times } enriched weight), whereas the literature values are calibrated in \ensuremath  {\mitmu }V; the comparison is therefore qualitative (relative ordering and graded response to precision) rather than a numerical match. A future calibration step would require fitting a per-subject \ensuremath  {\mitmu }V-per-nat scaling constant to empirical ERP data. Generated programmatically from \texttt {src.visualization.daif\_plots.plot\_erp\_predictions()}.}}{66}{figure.caption.27}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:daif-erp-predictions}{{26}{66}{Distributional prediction error predicts graded N400/P600 amplitudes across all eight case roles. \textbf {Left}: illustrative Gaussian ERP waveforms for three violation conditions --- congruent (NOM\texorpdfstring {\ensuremath {\to }}{->}NOM), mild (ACC\texorpdfstring {\ensuremath {\to }}{->}NOM), and strong (VOC\texorpdfstring {\ensuremath {\to }}{->}NOM) --- with fixed template amplitudes (\(-1.5/3.0\), \(-4.0/3.0\), \(-7.0/6.0\) \texorpdfstring {\ensuremath {\mu }}{mu}V N400/P600) and peaks at 380 ms / 600 ms (matching \protect \texttt {DEFAULT\_N400\_PEAK\_MS} / \protect \texttt {DEFAULT\_P600\_PEAK\_MS} in \protect \texttt {src/daif/prediction.py}), shown to depict the \emph {mechanism} of \autoref {eq:eq-7c-n400}--\autoref {eq:eq-7c-p600} rather than a calibrated simulation. \textbf {Middle}: scatter of enriched weight \(w\) versus scalar DPE (\autoref {eq:eq-7c-dpe-scalar}) for all eight case roles, computed via \protect \texttt {distributional\_prediction\_error()} in \protect \texttt {src/daif/prediction.py}. \textbf {Right}: \emph {real} DAIF-predicted magnitudes --- mean N400 magnitude via \protect \texttt {n400\_from\_return\_distribution()} and mean P600 via \protect \texttt {p600\_from\_precision\_update()} across the eight roles (\protect \texttt {make\_erp\_prediction\_data()} in \protect \texttt {src/cognitive/figure\_data.py}) --- alongside literature-typical amplitude ranges from Kutas \& Federmeier \citeyearpar {kutas2011thirty} (N400 magnitude 3--5 \texorpdfstring {\ensuremath {\mu }}{mu}V, P600 5--8 \texorpdfstring {\ensuremath {\mu }}{mu}V, shown with error bars). Note that the model predictions are on a \emph {dimensionless} DPE scale (units of log-probability \texorpdfstring {\ensuremath {\times }}{x} enriched weight), whereas the literature values are calibrated in \texorpdfstring {\ensuremath {\mu }}{mu}V; the comparison is therefore qualitative (relative ordering and graded response to precision) rather than a numerical match. A future calibration step would require fitting a per-subject \texorpdfstring {\ensuremath {\mu }}{mu}V-per-nat scaling constant to empirical ERP data. Generated programmatically from \protect \texttt {src.visualization.daif\_plots.plot\_erp\_predictions()}}{figure.caption.27}{}}
